Mark each arrow-like sprite's pointed frame endpoint with its color and the opposite endpoint black.

Input structure:
- The grid has a one-pixel solid rectangular frame around a dominant interior background.
- Inside the frame are monochrome six-pixel sprites shaped as asymmetric T arrows.
- Each sprite has a length-4 bar and a length-3 crossbar one cell closer to the pointed end.

Rule:
- For each sprite, follow its bar axis from the shorter end to the frame.
- Paint that frame endpoint with the sprite color.
- Paint the opposite endpoint on the same row or column black.
- Leave the interior background, sprites, and all other frame pixels unchanged.

Key insight:
- The direction is encoded by the crossbar's off-center position: the nearer end is the pointing end.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="da515329" lines="11-24">
<description>
Grow four clockwise spiral wall paths from the tips of a teal cross.

Input structure:
- bg = 0 (black). A single teal (8) cross centered in the grid.

Rule:
- Each arm tip launches a "turtle" heading 90° right of its outward direction.
- Movement priority: turn right > go straight > turn left (left only if not on border).
- Normal step = 2 cells; step right after a turn = 1 cell.
- Each new cell must have a 1-cell orthogonal gap from all existing walls except the predecessor.

Key insight: The right-turn preference plus alternating 2/1 step lengths creates self-avoiding spirals that tile the grid with uniform spacing.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="f28a3cbb" lines="42-50">
<description>
Compress each colored ray pixel inward until it touches that color's 3x3 anchor block.
Input structure
- A uniform background grid with non-background colors.
- Each foreground color contains one solid 3x3 anchor block; all other pixels of that color lie on straight 8-neighbor rays aimed at that block.
Rule
- Keep anchor blocks fixed, erase loose foreground pixels, and for each ray pack its pixels into consecutive cells adjacent to the anchor along the same ray.
Key insight
- The scattered pixels are displaced pixels on visible row, column, or diagonal sightlines back to their same-colored 3x3 block.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="d753a70b" lines="81-93">
<description>
Red diamond outlines contract by one layer and gray diamond outlines expand by one layer while other objects stay fixed.

Input structure
- A 16x16 orange-background grid contains one-cell-thick Manhattan-diamond outlines.
- Red and gray outlines are the changing objects; other colored diamond outlines are static.
- Some outlines may be clipped by the grid boundary, but their visible pixels still fit a clipped Manhattan-distance contour.
Rule
- Clear all red and gray pixels.
- Red objects are redrawn with the same diamond center and radius minus one.
- Gray objects are redrawn with radius plus one; an ambiguous edge-clipped growing arc continues away from the nearest perpendicular edge.
Key insight
- The task is not about moving whole objects: each changing object is a contour of a diamond, and the visible operation is one step of contour contraction or expansion.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="a644e277" lines="31-50">
<description>
Extract the lattice subregion bounded by the four background-colored corner holes.

Input structure:
- A regular one-cell-thick lattice has one line color, one background color,
  and sparse marker pixels.
- Exactly four lattice-line intersections are background-colored holes instead
  of line-color pixels.

Rule:
- Find the unique non-background color that forms regular horizontal and
  vertical lattice lines.
- Use the four background-colored lattice intersections as the corners of one
  rectangle.
- Return the inclusive crop between those corner rows and columns.

Key insight:
- The crop is marked by the missing lattice intersections, not by the marker
  pixels inside the cells.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="80214e03" lines="48-64">
<description>
Extract the color of each noisy block in a row/column lattice and mirror the coarse grid horizontally.

Input structure:
- A background surrounds a rectangular lattice of monochrome foreground blocks.
- Consecutive rows in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.
- Consecutive columns in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.
- Each row-band/column-band intersection has one foreground color, with possible background holes.

Rule:
- Group non-empty rows and columns into consecutive bands by their foreground color sets.
- Replace each lattice cell by the single foreground color inside that row/column-band intersection.
- Return the resulting coarse color grid with the column order reversed.

Key insight:
- Whole-row and whole-column color sets reveal the hidden lattice even when neighboring cells share a color or contain holes.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="182e5d0f" lines="89-103">
<description>
Move each gray marker along its green path until it reaches the cell next to a black-green-black gate, erasing the path behind it.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Foreground components are made from a path color, a paired gate-end color, and single marker pixels.
- A gate is a straight end-center-end motif whose center has the path color and whose two ends share the gate-end color.

Rule:
- In every 4-connected foreground component with exactly one marker and one gate, erase the marker and all path-color pixels except the gate center.
- Place the marker on the unique path-color pixel adjacent to the gate center but not between the two gate ends.
- Components without both a marker and a gate stay unchanged.

Key insight: The black-green-black motif marks the stopping gate; the gray marker consumes the connected green path until it rests on the path cell immediately beside that gate.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="eval" puzzle_id="67e490f4" lines="8-22">
<description>
Fill holes inside a large template frame using shape-matched outside sprites.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. One large monochrome frame object (template).
- Inside the frame: holes of various shapes (non-frame-color regions).
- Outside the frame: small monochrome sprites scattered on bg.

Rule:
- Crop to the frame's bounding box; fill entirely with frame color.
- For each hole, find an outside sprite whose shape matches (rotation/flip allowed).
- Fill the hole with the matched sprite's color. If multiple sprites match, use the most frequent color.

Key insight: Sprites act as shape-coded color keys — their shape identifies which hole they fill, their color determines the fill color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="14" source="eval" puzzle_id="edb79dae" lines="39-53">
<description>
Stamp recolored sprites into a framed region using placeholder positions and a color legend.

Input structure:
- bg background, a rectangular single-color frame, colored placeholder cells inside the frame.
- Outside the frame: sprite shapes (one per placeholder color) and a small legend mapping each placeholder color to an output color.

Rule:
- Find the frame and its interior placeholders.
- For each placeholder color, locate the matching-color sprite outside the frame.

